A||||13||Kardien||Stuck thermostat housing cap||||1||
Z||000000||Kardien||12-19-2001||07:57 PM||||Changing the anti-freeze seemed like it'd be a trival job...until I couldn't get the thermostat housing cap off!<P>The car is an 88 Formula with the stock 2.8. The cap and housing both appear to be in decent shape, except for a trace amount of rust around the housing and some scratches on the cap itself. It looks like the previous owners may have has this problem as well.<P>I could use every suggestion you have. I'm supposed to be leaving for Wisconsin in 12 hours--hence why I'm changing the coolant in my Florida car--and I'd really like to be sure about my coolant.<P>Kardien||||reg||1
Z||000001||avengador1||12-19-2001||08:33 PM||||Use a small oil filter wrench or the strap thing sold a Sears. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||12-19-2001||08:38 PM||||Those caps are usually a bi***h to get off.  Check your antifreeze from the coolant over flow tank or radiator to see how good/bad it is.  I used a pipe wrench to get one off once.  It gave me a lot of torque to twist it off.  I think you'll need to push down on it while you rotate it.  Sort of like a child proof top on medicine bottles.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||12-19-2001||10:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by avengador1:<BR><B>Use a small oil filter wrench or the strap thing sold a Sears.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Those strap wrenches are great!  I highly recommend using one to remove the thermostat cap.....if you use a pipe wrench or whatever on it you run the risk of bending or flexing the cap to where it will not seal properly when you reinstall it.<P>Don't forget to put in a new thermostat while you have the cap off!<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000004||Jefrysuko||12-20-2001||12:52 AM||||When I have one that is hard to get off I use a screwdriver and put it on the part that it hanging down around the lip of the housing and tap it off with a hammer.  I have yet to dammage one doing this but I do usually go and get a new one because they are only a few dollars.||||reg||1
Z||000005||SKIDMARK||12-20-2001||01:22 AM||||The cap is cheap, the housing is expensive. Try tapping on the cap with a soft mallet a few times to jar things loose. I had the same problem and just a little tapping got it off. Whatever you do, don't damage the housing. It's expensive and hard to find (voice of experience). <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Blacktree||12-20-2001||01:26 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Use a small oil filter wrench...<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Ditto. Works like a charm.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||12-20-2001||07:49 AM||||I got some of those "grippy" gloves from Home Depot. Cloth, with a rubber grain in the palms. Still have to use plenty of muscle, though. <BR>Haven't tried the oil filter wrench, though. <BR>Sounds easier.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||deceler8||12-20-2001||09:17 AM||||When you get it off, replace it. When they get that hard to remove, the gasket is usually bad.<P>To keep it from sticking again, spray Armorall or Son of a Gun on the gasket before reinstalling it. Makes it easier to put back on and keeps it from getting stuck.||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||12-20-2001||11:25 AM||||I use the ole pipe wrench too||||reg||1
Z||000010||84Bill||12-20-2001||12:08 PM||||I have found that if I push down hard on the cap and turn at the same time does the trick. I put a light coat of vasoline on the gasket as well.||||reg||14
Z||000011||Pyric||12-20-2001||12:22 PM||||I have a big set of adjustable pliers and works too.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Kardien||12-21-2001||01:32 AM||||Thanks for the suggestions! I went to Sears looking for a strap wrench, but they were sold out. I tried a small oil filter wrench and it worked BEAUTIFULLY! The cap came off without effort.<P>And yes--the cap's gasket was in very poor shape.<P>Thanks again for the advice. I was shocked at how well the filter wrench worked.||||reg||1
Z||000013||kempo||12-21-2001||11:10 AM||||I didn't have an oil filter wrench, so I used a pipe wrench =)||||reg||1
